  "summary": "Predictive models now link structural contact probabilities and few-shot sequence learning to measurable CRISPR base and indel editing outcomes.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational prediction applied to CRISPR editing outcomes, with a predictor coupled to an experimental readout. ContactSeek derives AlphaFold3-predicted contact probabilities and correlates them with off-target sequencing data to identify specificity-determining residues in DNA base editing. inDecay predicts CRISPR-induced indel proportions from target sequence using a parameter-efficient, cell-type-aware few-shot architecture, and is evaluated across somatic cell lines and zygotes. EXCAVATE-HT is a related but distinct case: it mines population variant data to design allele-specific gRNA libraries and excision pairs, illustrated on CRX. The new element is linking predicted structural contacts or sequence-level representations to functional editing readouts and to interpretable design outputs.",
